"title": "Valuation, Owner Earnings, and Business Quality",
"lead": {
"text": "Evaluating a stock requires assessing whether a business is understandable, possesses favorable long-term prospects, is managed by competent people, and is available at an attractive price. Investors must reject the \"cigar butt\" approach of buying mediocre businesses cheaply and instead focus on wonderful companies at fair prices. The evaluation process hinges on understanding owner earnings, which differ from reported GAAP earnings, and recognizing the economic differences between franchises and standard businesses.",
"citations": [
"P01-001",
"P01-004",
"P01-011",
"P01-012",
"P14-004",
"P14-007"
]
},
"sections": [
{
"heading": "The Framework of Valuation and Margin of Safety",
"paragraphs": [
"An investor should evaluate a stock using the same criteria used to acquire a business in its entirety. This involves seeking a business that is understandable, has favorable long-term prospects, is run by honest and competent management, and is available at an attractive price. While \"value\" and \"growth\" are often seen as opposing schools of thought, growth is actually a variable component of the value calculation. The ultimate value of a stock or bond is determined by the present value of all cash inflows and outflows expected during the remaining life of the asset, discounted at an appropriate interest rate. The cheapest investment to purchase is the one with the highest calculated value, regardless of its growth profile or volatility. To manage the risk of estimation errors, investors must insist on a margin of safety in their purchase price; a calculated value that is only slightly higher than the current price is generally not sufficient.",
"citations": [
"P01-001",
"P01-003",
"P01-004",
"P01-008",
"P01-009",
"P01-012"
]
}
]
},
{
"heading": "Economic Franchises vs. Standard Businesses",
"paragraphs": [
"An economic franchise exists when a product or service is needed, has no close substitute in the eyes of customers, and is not subject to price regulation. Franchises demonstrate the ability to price aggressively and earn high returns on capital, and they are resilient enough to tolerate mismanagement. In contrast, a standard business earns exceptional profits only if it is a low-cost operator or if supply is tight, a condition that usually does not last. Standard businesses are highly vulnerable to poor management and constant competitive attacks. It is far better to own a wonderful business than a fair one, as a first-class business will succeed with decent management, whereas a broken-down business will fail even under the best jockey. Investors should identify one-foot hurdles they can step over rather than trying to solve difficult business problems.",
"citations": [
"P15-004",
"P15-005",
"P14-005",
"P14-007",
"P14-008",
"P14-010"
]
}
]
},
{
"heading": "Calculating Owner Earnings",
"paragraphs": [
"Reported GAAP earnings are often inadequate for valuation because they fail to account for capital maintenance requirements. The relevant measure for valuation is \"owner earnings,\" which is calculated by taking reported earnings and adding back depreciation, depletion, and amortization, then subtracting the average annual amount of capitalized expenditures required to maintain the business's long-term competitive position and unit volume. While the owner earnings equation yields a less precise number than GAAP figures, it represents the cash a business can actually generate. Standard \"cash flow\" numbers found in Wall Street reports are often misleading because they add back depreciation without subtracting the maintenance capital expenditures needed to keep the business operational. In many cases, the historical depreciation figure is a closer approximation to these maintenance costs than the inflated post-acquisition figures used in GAAP reporting.",
"citations": [
"P10-133",
"P10-134",
"P10-137",
"P10-138",
"P10-142",
"P10-144"
]
}
]
},
{
"heading": "The Impact of Growth and Management",
"paragraphs": [
"Growth is beneficial to investors only when the business can invest at incremental returns that are high, creating more than a dollar of long-term value for every dollar used to finance the growth. Conversely, growth in a low-return business destroys investor value. This highlights why a business that can employ large amounts of capital at very high rates of return is the best business to own, while those requiring constant large capital outlays for low returns are poor businesses. Furthermore, the quality of management significantly affects equity \"coupons,\" unlike bonds where management has little impact on the fixed coupon. Investors must also recognize the \"institutional imperative,\" where corporate dynamics cause managers to mindlessly imitate peers or resist change, often leading to poor decisions regardless of the manager's intelligence.",
"citations": [
"P01-007",
"P01-008",
"P01-010",
"P14-012",
"P14-013"
]
}
]
}
]
}
